I want you to take a moment and picture  the person who gave you your first big  career opportunity that person who took  a chance on you to do something you've  never done before  maybe before you are really ready do you  remember their name what they looked  like what that meant for you so I don't  know who you're picturing once their  name is or what they look like but I do  know something important about them in  some meaningful way they changed your  life now often when we talk about people  like this we call them Mentors  and we know that mentors are people who  give us advice share perspectives teach  us new skills and we're taught that  early in our career we should be  constantly scanning our working  environment always on the hunt for that  next mentor but not everyone who mentors  you has the power to help you move up to  get to that next level to advocate for  you when it really counts we need a  different word for that we'll call that  sponsorship and while I believe that  mentors matter we need sponsors to be  really successful so what is sponsorship  a sponsor sees the potential in you they  give you access to the kinds of  experiences and relationships that they  already have but you may not be able to  find on your own  and finally they'll put their own  reputation on the line to advocate for  your promotion whether it's to greater  responsibility or visibility now my  guess is that each of you could probably  tell me right now specifically  what your sponsor did for you but  sponsorship as a skill set is  generalizable it matters in every  profession so to picture this I want you  to picture a professional hero of mine  Yoda  now I loved Star Wars as a kid and I  loved it even more now because Yoda it  turns out is one of the greatest  sponsors of all time and here's why  when we first meet him Luke Skywalker  looks a lot more like a farmhand than a  Jedi Master  but Yoda sees through that to the  potential in him and decides to sponsor  him next he creates opportunities for  Luke to learn the kinds of skills he'll  need to be successful as a Jedi now  what's important about this is that he's  not just giving Luke advice he's giving  him access to the kinds of experiences  that Luke wouldn't have known how to  find on his own and finally and if you  haven't already been taking this  metaphor with a grain of salt you could  start now  Yodas dying act is to promote Luke he  makes him the last of the living Jedi  and that former farmhand goes on to save  the galaxy so at this point you might  think that this talk is about the  importance of finding your Yoda someone  to advocate for you and help you achieve  great things but actually this is a talk  about the importance of being a Yoda and  how to discover that greatness in others  and there's three important reasons to  do so first and this might be obvious  the sponsorship is a way to have a  measurable impact on somebody else's  life think back to the person you  pictured at the beginning you know what  a powerful impact that sponsorship had  for you but what if you would never met  that person or what if you had but they  just hadn't chosen you to take that  chance on where would you be now  that gap between what could have been  and what is is tangible to you although  perhaps hard to measure except looking  back so if you want to change lives  sponsorship is a way to fill that gap  for someone else now second and this is  perhaps less intuitive sponsorship turns  out to be good for the sponsor imagine  having a bench of people who feel about  you the way you feel about the person  you pictured what could you get done and  this isn't fluffy stuff a Deloitte study  found that 70% of people who  self-identify as sponsors reported  faster progression in their own careers  and this makes sense right because while  early in our careers were rewarded for  being strong individual producers there  comes a time when the things were asked  to achieve we can't do on our own  and that's when having a bench count and  there's something powerful about filling  that bench with people who are early in  their own careers there's a saying in  Silicon Valley that there's two kinds of  investors here people who invest in the  face book early and people who didn't  but wish they did now I can't tell you  how to invest your money but when you  invest in people early the return is a  network that's not just broader but  deeper and that social network is a  competitive advantage as a leader who  wants to get big things done so  sponsorships it's good for others and  it's good for you but there's a third  reason I want you to consider this and  that's because sponsorship is a powerful  tool for changing organizations from the  inside out no matter what stage you're  at in your own career and here's why we  know that organizations with diverse  teams have better business outcomes and  when I look at the world outside of our  classrooms I see lots of organizations  that have figured out how to hire fairly  diverse pools of talent at least at the  entry level but despite knowing this  her ship remains looking stubbornly like  it did 30 years ago this says to me that  there's something broken and how we're  moving people through organizations and  that it looks less like a ladder than  like a climbing wall where the ropes and  carabiners are only handed down to some  people I believe that in many ways it  comes down to who is getting sponsored  research shows that we're more likely to  spend time with communicate with trust  and by extension sponsor people who are  similar to us  it's literally human nature but in a  world where 80% of senior leaders and  97% of CEOs are men it's perhaps not  surprising that sponsorship can end up  looking in some ways like a  self-replicating system and again people  liking people who are like them doesn't  make them bad people just human but what  it says is that if we truly want to  create diverse organizations we have to  do what feels unnatural and be  intentional about looking for potential  and people who don't look like us no  matter who you are or what you look like  I hope the bench of people you build  looks less like this and more like this  so I hope that by being explicit about  what sponsorship is what it's done for  you and me and how we can step into that  role ourselves we could be the one to  break that self-replicating system and  it's not hard to do first we can start  now  so many of us picture our sponsors the  older and wiser but you don't have to  wait until you're a shriveled green 900  year old to do this no matter where you  are there are Luke Skywalker's behind  you wanting to go to your academic  institution work for your company or  move up into your current role we can be  intentional about looking for potential  and people who don't look like us we can  be Salsa about giving those people  access to the experiences and  relationships we already have that they  may not know how to find on their own  and finally we can be strategic about  advocating for their promotion so they  can benefit from our own attempts to  make it up that climbing wall to change  the world that we inherit we mean not  just more sponsorship but better  sponsorship and we need it to start now  not in another decade so I hope that  this top could be a kind of playbook for  us to go out into the world and be  intentional about sponsoring and I hope  you do so because it's a powerful way to  have an impact on somebody else's life  and I hope you do so because it's an  investment in yourself and your ability  to get big things done and I hope you do  so because it's a powerful tool for  changing organizations from the inside  out but most of all I hope you do so  because our legacies are not just the  businesses and bank accounts we leave  behind but rather the people whose lives  we've helped to build you know what it's  like to have found Euro to so go out and  find your L